				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/themightymcb" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4483018); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4483018&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606380111" alt="TheMightyMcB" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4483018)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/themightymcb" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4483018); return false;" >TheMightyMcB</a></span> <span class="odate time_1556038392 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Apr 2019 16:53</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4227601">
									

<p>Noticed this didn't have too many eyes on it, and wasn't very highly rated either.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Line&nbsp;by&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Line&nbsp;by&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] at coordinates ██° ██′ ██″ N ██° ██′ ██″ E.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Seeing blackboxes in the containment procedures is kind of a major red flag for many veteran users. How can you contain something if information about it is being hidden from you?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to risk of personnel loss</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Due to <em>the</em> risk of personnel loss</p>
<blockquote>
<p>two (2)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is another red flag for SCP vets. Most scientific writing would simply have you write out numbers 20 and below, and use the characters for anything larger. So there would be <strong>eight</strong> fish and <strong>45</strong> dolphins, for example.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>an estimated ██ humans</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is this number redacted? You could give an approximation, like &quot;two dozen&quot;, or something like that. There's no in-universe reason to hide this number from the researchers reading this. I suggest checking out <a href="/zen-and-the-art-of-data-expunged">Zen and the Art of DATA EXPUNGED</a> for more information regarding proper redaction etiquette.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>living organisms requiring oxygen are able to breathe normally</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All aerobic organisms require oxygen, including marine animals. You might want to specify that specifically air breathing organisms can breathe down there to avoid confusion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is sapient, though unintelligent, [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you'd be better served to just say that it is no more intelligent than most other non-anomalous octopus species.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Various chemical treatments, including [REDACTED] have been successful in alleviating the effects of SCP-4909-2 on individuals retrieved from SCP-4909, though symptoms of depression have followed in █ of the █ cases recorded to date.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Kind of beating the dead horse here, but again, in-universe personnel would need to know what to give their comrades who are affected by SCP-4909-2.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is made of a material similar to steel, but appears to be indestructible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't tell us anything. You're better off mentioning the indestructability in the previous sentence, not here on its own.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The following interview was obtained from █████ █████,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You really should come up with a name. Sites like <a href="https://www.behindthename.com/random/">https://www.behindthename.com/random/</a> are great for this. Much easier to identify with a person with a name than it is to identify with █████ █████.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>being taken to SCPS-0215’s medical bay.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Best to avoid possessives when you can. Maybe something like &quot;taken to the medical bay of SCPS-0215.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dialogue translated to English from Hindi.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sentence fragment. You need a &quot;has been&quot; in there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Further research revealed that SCP-4909 and SCP-4909-3 bear a beyond-coincidental similarity to lyrics contained in The Beatles' songs “Octopus’s Garden” and “Yellow Submarine,” respectively.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh my god was this whole thing a giant Beatles pun? That actually almost makes me want to downvote right there. Reminds me of like those 4chan stories that get you hooked until the final line is something stupid like &quot;and his name was john cena&quot;. I don't mind the subtle Beatles references of an octopus garden and a yellow submarine, but beating the reader over the head with it might not be the best idea.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Mobile Task Force MTF Omega-9 (“Lonely Hearts Club”)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Honestly this is tongue-in-cheek enough for me to be completely cool with it, but Christ that earlier stuff literally drawing a straight Beatles connection was too much.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I understand this is an atypical criticism of anyone [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You might wanna restructure the collapsible to not be in quoteblocks so that notes like these can be. Just a personal stylistic thing, but that's ultimately up to you.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, I have four major gripes with this:</p>
<ol>
<li>The needless and constant redaction is annoying, not gonna lie.</li>
<li>Your description is HUGE. Trimming it down would improve the pacing dramatically.</li>
<li>The ham-fisted delivery of the Beatles references killed any impact or realization that it otherwise might have had on my part.</li>
<li>This is the big one I think. You relied way too heavily on <a href="/scp-1485">SCP-1485</a> to tell your story. It's one thing to have crosslinks in the article. It's another entirely to have an article be the entire basis of your big reveal. I had to stop reading your piece to read 1485, and then come back when I was done, and that destroyed all of the pacing and tension you'd built up to that point, including the interview that took place just before it.</li>
</ol>
<p>Given all of that, I've left a downvote. Hope this can be helpful for your future articles though!</p>

